{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "log-05-common-logs-known-values",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Common Logarithms from Known Values",
    "description": "Use known common-log values such as log 2 and log 3 to calculate logs of related numbers without a table.",
    "teachingApproach": "Treat known common logs as building blocks. Rewrite each target number using 2, 3, 5, 7, and powers of 10, then use the log laws. Keep the decimal arithmetic visible and avoid table language unless the value is supplied.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 10, Logarithms. Use the chapter convention that an omitted base is 10. Starting from supplied values such as log 2=0.3010, log 3=0.4771, and log 7=0.8451, derive log 5=1−log2 and then values such as log25, log50, log80, log1.5, log2.8, and log60000. Include change-of-base evaluations such as log_5 512. Keep exact decimal arithmetic visible and explain how rewriting a number as a product, quotient, or power makes the known logs useful."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's calculate new common logarithms from a few known ones.\n\nIf log 2 is given, many other logs can be built by rewriting the target number as a product, quotient, or power.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A very useful partner of 2 is 5 because 2×5=10.\n\nlog2 + log5 = log10 = 1.\nSo if log2=0.3010, then log5=1−0.3010=0.6990.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "This is faster than looking up log5 separately.\n\nWhenever a number pairs with another to make a power of 10, the product law gives a shortcut.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
      "content": {
        "text": "If log 2 = 0.30103, what is log 5?",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
